	abstract = {Sign Language Recognition (SLR) enables communication between Deaf individuals and the wider community, presenting a critical technological solution. This research focuses on isolated signer-independent SLR using Spatio-Temporal Graph Convolutional Networks (ST-GCNs), which leverage pose estimation to enhance recognition accuracy across various signers. A novel approach is proposed, combining MediaPipe Holistic for human pose estimation with ST-GCN to model both spatial and temporal dimensions of sign language. The system is evaluated on two datasets: LSA64 and AUTSL. Our method achieves 91.25\% accuracy on the LSA64 dataset and 88.95\% on the more complex AUTSL dataset. Ablation studies further demonstrate the impact of frame selection and landmark post-processing on model performance. These results highlight the efficiency of ST-GCNs in achieving robust isolated signer-independent SLR, outperforming previous unimodal methods.},
