	doi = {10.1145/3723178.3723214},
	abstract = {The twenty-first century saw the introduction of new technologies that can aid communication and assist people with disabilities. For instance, using a mechanical hand to convert sign language into English was first showcased in 1977. Such innovations fueled inventions that could assist sign language users with communication. Our research, in simple terms, aims at overcoming the inability to verbalize among the deaf members of a society. With this in mind, we propose a system based on Natural language processing (NLPs) that should be able to recognize characteristics associated with deafness and interpret them into comprehensible language. We augment our dataset with 102 Bangladeshi Sign Language (BdSL) samples. We tried several models, including custom LSTM models, ResNet-50, and SSD MobileNet V2, but distinctly, the LSTM Model gives an average accuracy of 96.19\%. Other researchers can retrieve our model and revise the dataset in different languages. In conclusion, our research promotes the agenda of “Communication for everyone”.},
